<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dmitry Korybut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">after 1350–after 1404</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Dmitry Koribut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dmitry Korybut was a Lithuanian prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminid dynasty</a>, a son of Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a>, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a> in the late 14th century. He was baptised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> faith and took part in the dynastic struggles of his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> and Vytautas, sometimes in revolt. His son Sigismund Korybut later led Hussite forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmitry-korybut.jpg" alt="Dmitry Korybut" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaributas (after 1350 – after 1404) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Kaributas_with_Lithuanian_Vytis_(Waykimas),_1386.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
